{
  "messages": [
    {
      "time": "09:15",
      "author": "Timo Laine",
      "role": "Field Engineer",
      "text": "Moi Erkki! I just replaced the SFP on TRN-019 here at OULU-03. The link is physically up and optics look normal, but all traffic counters are staying at zero."
    },
    {
      "time": "09:18",
      "author": "Erkki Expert",
      "role": "Network Expert",
      "text": "Moi Timo. Ah, that is one of those old TX-200 units from 2016. They have this weird bug sometimes with new SFPs."
    },
    {
      "time": "09:19",
      "author": "Erkki Expert",
      "role": "Network Expert",
      "text": "Try to disable the interface, wait about 30 seconds, and then enable it again. Don't reboot the node, the port bounce usually does the trick."
    },
    {
      "time": "09:22",
      "author": "Timo Laine",
      "role": "Field Engineer",
      "text": "Okay, I will try that now. Shutting down the port."
    },
    {
      "time": "09:28",
      "author": "Timo Laine",
      "role": "Field Engineer",
      "text": "I tried the bounce but it didn't help at all. The port disable/enable trick completely failed to clear the issue and counters were still stuck."
    },
    {
      "time": "09:31",
      "author": "Timo Laine",
      "role": "Field Engineer",
      "text": "I had to reboot the node in the end. After the reboot, TRN-019 is working fine and traffic is flowing normally now."
    },
    {
      "time": "09:34",
      "author": "Erkki Expert",
      "role": "Network Expert",
      "text": "Hm, so the bounce didn't work on that one, odd. Usually it is enough, but good that the reboot fixed it anyway!"
    }
  ]
}
